The quick-sort algorithm in Java is implemented using the divide and conquer technique. It involves selecting a pivot element and partitioning the array around it. Here is an example implementation in Java:

```
public class QuickSort {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] array = {9, 5, 1, 8, 3, 2, 7};
        quickSort(array, 0, array.length - 1);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(array));
    }

    public static void quickSort(int[] array, int start, int end) {
        if (start < end) {
            int pivotIndex = partition(array, start, end);
            quickSort(array, start, pivotIndex - 1);
            quickSort(array, pivotIndex + 1, end);
        }
    }

    public static int partition(int[] array, int start, int end) {
        int pivot = array[end];
        int i = start - 1;
        for (int j = start; j < end; j++) {
            if (array[j] < pivot) {
                i++;
                swap(array, i, j);
            }
        }
        swap(array, i + 1, end);
        return i + 1;
    }

    public static void swap(int[] array, int i, int j) {
        int temp = array[i];
        array[i] = array[j];
        array[j] = temp;
    }
}
```

In this example, the `quickSort` method is called to sort the `array` by specifying the starting and ending indices. The `partition` method is responsible for selecting the pivot, partitioning the array, and returning the index of the pivot element. The `swap` method is used to swap elements within the array. The process is repeated recursively until the array is fully sorted.

