Here's an example C++ program that implements a bubble sort algorithm with flag technique to sort an array of integers:

```
#include <iostream>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <ctime>

using namespace std;

int main()
{
    int size;
    cout << "Enter the size of the array: ";
    cin >> size;

    int arr[size], temp;
    cout << "Populating the array with " << size << " random integers..." << endl;
    srand(time(NULL));
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        arr[i] = rand() % 100;
    }

    cout << "Unsorted array: ";
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;

    int comparisons = 0, swaps = 0;
    bool flag = true;
    for (int i = 0; i < size - 1 && flag; i++) {
        flag = false;
        for (int j = 0; j < size - i - 1; j++) {
            comparisons++;
            if (arr[j] > arr[j+1]) {
                temp = arr[j];
                arr[j] = arr[j+1];
                arr[j+1] = temp;
                swaps++;
                flag = true;
            }
        }
    }

    cout << "Sorted array: ";
    for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
        cout << arr[i] << " ";
    }
    cout << endl;

    cout << "Number of comparisons: " << comparisons << endl;
    cout << "Number of swaps: " << swaps << endl;

    return 0;
}
```

In this program, we first prompt the user to enter the size of the array. We then populate the array with random integers using the `srand()` and `rand()` functions from the `<cstdlib>` library. We display the unsorted array, and then implement the bubble sort algorithm with flag technique using two nested loops.

In each iteration of the outer loop, we set the `flag` variable to `false`, which means that we assume the array is sorted unless we find a pair of adjacent elements that are out of order. In each iteration of the inner loop, we compare each pair of adjacent elements and swap them if they are out of order. If we make any swaps during an iteration, we set the `flag` variable to `true`, which means that we need to continue sorting the array.

After sorting the array, we display the sorted array and the number of comparisons and swaps made during the sorting process.

