The quicksort algorithm is a sorting algorithm that follows a divide and conquer approach. It selects a pivot element from the array and partitions the other elements into two sub-arrays based on whether they are less than or greater than the pivot.

Here are the steps involved in the quicksort algorithm:

1. Selecting the pivot: The first step is to choose a pivot element from the array. This can be done randomly or strategically, like always selecting the middle element. The performance of the algorithm can be influenced by the choice of pivot.

2. Partitioning: After selecting the pivot, the array is rearranged so that all elements with values less than the pivot come before it, and all elements with values greater than the pivot come after it. This is the key step in the quicksort algorithm and is achieved using a partitioning process.

3. Recursive process: The partitioning step creates two sub-arrays: one with elements less than the pivot and another with elements greater than the pivot. The quicksort algorithm is then applied recursively to both sub-arrays. This recursion continues until the sub-array size becomes zero or one, which is the base case (as an array of size zero or one is already sorted).

Here is the pseudo-code for the quicksort algorithm:

```pseudo
function quicksort(array, low, high) 
{
    if (low < high) 
    {
        pi = partition(array, low, high);
        quicksort(array, low, pi - 1); // Before pi
        quicksort(array, pi + 1, high); // After pi
    }
}

function partition(array, low, high) 
{
    pivot = array[high];  
    i = (low - 1) // Index of the smaller element

    for (j = low; j <= high- 1; j++) 
    {
        if (array[j] < pivot) 
        {
            i++; // Increment the index of the smaller element
            swap array[i] and array[j]
        }
    }
    swap array[i + 1] and array[high]) 
    return (i + 1); 
}
```

The time complexity of the quicksort algorithm in the average case is O(n log n), which means it efficiently sorts large datasets. However, in the worst case (when the array is already sorted or reverse sorted), the time complexity can increase to O(n^2). The choice of pivot is critical for achieving the best performance. Ideally, the pivot should be selected to be close to the median of the array elements.

One advantage of quicksort is that it is an in-place sorting algorithm, which means it doesn't require any extra storage apart from the call stack. This makes it memory-efficient.

